      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Base rate/reference class: for monthly national WIC total participation, the appropriate base rate is persistence plus recent monthly drift in the same FNS table, not broader SNAP or annual WIC quality-control releases. The late-2025 level was about 6.74 million, while the 2025 spring value for the same month-of-year neighborhood was about 6.76 million."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence prior = latest official 2025-11 value 6.739 million; historical sample = same-series monthly values 2025-05 through 2025-11 plus FY annual averages. Seasonal adjustment = +0.023 million because 2025-05 was 6.762 and 2025-11 was 6.739. Trend adjustment = +0.028 million, using the FY 2024 to FY 2025 annual-average increase of 6.760 - 6.697 = 0.063 million and applying about 5.3/12 of that annual pace to the late-2025-to-May horizon. Point = 6.739 + 0.023 + 0.028 = 6.790 million. For recent successive monthly changes (+0.010, +0.009, -0.004, -0.016, -0.013, -0.009 plus earlier same-table monthly moves), sigma = 0.033 million; 1.28*sigma = 0.042 million. I widen to 0.170 million because this is a six-month-ahead first-print forecast and WIC enrollment can shift with administrative recertification and outreach, so the 80% interval is 6.79 +/- 0.17 = [6.62, 6.96]."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Counter-considerations: upside risk is a stronger-than-expected enrollment rebound from outreach, food-cost pressure, or state processing improvements, which would land above the interval if May 2026 exceeds 6.96 million. Downside risk is faster normalization of caseloads, funding disruptions, or administrative churn, which would land below the interval if May 2026 is under 6.62 million."
      },
